-- title:
--   Existence of a surjection R ↠ T
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a complete discrete valuation domain (a commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring and is adically complete for its maximal ideal), let $\bar\rho$ be a residual Galois representation over the residue field of $\mathcal{O}$ — in the project's sense: a $2$-dimensional representation space for $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ (realised as $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphisms of `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`) whose action factors through a finite level — and let $\mathcal{D}$ be any predicate on adic Galois representations over local $\mathcal{O}$-algebras. Suppose given $D$, a [`GaloisRep.DeformationRingData`](def/GaloisRep_DeformationRingData.html#L8) for $(\mathcal{O},\bar\rho,\mathcal{D})$: a ring $D.R$ which is local, Noetherian, adically complete, an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra via a local homomorphism whose composition with the residue map is surjective, together with absolute irreducibility of $\bar\rho$, a representation $D.\rho$ of type $\mathcal{D}$ whose residual representation is equivalent to the base change of $\bar\rho$ along the induced residue-field map, and the universal property: for every local Noetherian adically complete $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $A$ with local structure map and surjective residue composite and every $\rho_A$ of type $\mathcal{D}$ with residual representation equivalent to the base change of $\bar\rho$, there is a unique $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\varphi : D.R \to A$ which is local and along which $D.\rho$ base-changes to a representation equivalent to $\rho_A$. Suppose further given $N \geq 1$, a set $S$ of naturals, a ring homomorphism $\theta$ from the weight-$2$ level-$N$ Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) to the residue field of $\mathcal{O}$, a ring $T$ that is local, Noetherian, adically complete, an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra via a local homomorphism, and finite and free as an $\mathcal{O}$-module, and $H$, a [`CuspForm.HeckeGaloisRepDatum N S 𝒪 θ T`](def/CuspForm_HeckeGaloisRepDatum.html#L8): a ring map $\pi$ from the Hecke algebra to $T$ reducing to $\theta$ modulo the maximal ideal, with $\mathcal{O}$-algebra generated by the image of $\pi$, with the property that every $\mathcal{O}$-valued Hecke character reducing to $\theta$ factors through $T$ by an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map, with surjective residue composite, and with a representation $H.\rho$ over $T$ whose Frobenius characteristic polynomials at primes $\ell \nmid N$, $\ell \notin S$ are $X^{2} - \pi(T_\ell)X + \ell$ and whose residual representation is absolutely irreducible. Assume finally that every prime dividing $N$ lies in $S$, that $H.\rho$ is of type $\mathcal{D}$, and that the residual representation of $H.\rho$ is equivalent to the base change of $\bar\rho$ along the residue-field map induced by $\mathcal{O} \to T$. Then there exists an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra homomorphism $\varphi : D.R \to T$ which is surjective and is a local homomorphism, and such that the base change of $D.\rho$ along $\varphi$ is equivalent to $H.\rho$. No uniqueness of $\varphi$ is asserted in the conclusion, and nothing is claimed about $\varphi$ being an isomorphism.
--
--   This is the first half of Wiles's "$R = T$": the classifying map from the universal deformation ring of $\bar\rho$ to the local Hecke algebra exists and is onto, because the Hecke algebra is generated by traces of Frobenius. Compared with the textbook statement, both sides here are axiomatised by the project's structures — [`GaloisRep.DeformationRingData`](def/GaloisRep_DeformationRingData.html#L8) packages a universal deformation ring with a deformation condition $\mathcal{D}$ given as an abstract predicate, and [`CuspForm.HeckeGaloisRepDatum`](def/CuspForm_HeckeGaloisRepDatum.html#L8) packages a ring with a Hecke map, an interpolation property and Eichler–Shimura characteristic polynomials — so the result is a statement about arbitrary inhabitants of those structures, and it provides only the surjection, not the isomorphism. It is the input to the Taylor–Wiles patching step, being used in the construction of patching data and hence in the two modularity lifting theorems at $p = 3$ and $p \in \{3,5\}$ for semistable integral Weierstrass models.
